    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is additive and consistent with the established conventions of this codebase.\n\n- **Logic is in the right unit.** `DeleteOwner` stays a single-method soft-delete step (flag + save), and the three create-time scans each keep their own scan predicate. That matches the OfficeFloor idiom documented in AGENTS.md: one small, self-contained function class per step.\n\n- **The repeated `Boolean.TRUE.equals(existing.getDeleted())` guard is the right call, not a smell to extract.** Centralizing the \"ignore deleted\" filter at the repository (`findAll`) would be an over-reach \u2014 `findAll()` is used by read/list paths that may legitimately want every row, so each create-check call site deciding for itself is the safer, more correct placement. The per-site decision is genuinely different logic context (household duplicate vs. namesake count vs. possible-duplicate), even if the one-line guard text coincides.\n\n- **Null-safe idiom matches existing code.** The codebase uses plain `Boolean` getters with `Boolean.TRUE.equals(...)` at call sites (e.g. `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique` already does this for `getSharesHousehold()`, and `Owner` exposes `getPossibleDuplicate()`/`getBulkSignupWarning()` as plain Boolean getters with no `isX()` convenience methods). Adding an `Owner.isDeleted()` helper would actually diverge from the established pattern; the inline guard is the natural choice here. The null-safe form also correctly handles pre-existing rows.\n\n- **Supporting changes are standard and complete.** Entity field with a sensible `false` default + accessors, matching `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE` across all four DB dialects, and a `readOnly` OpenAPI property with an accurate description. No class or method is becoming a catch-all.\n\nA maintainer of this repo would find this natural.",
